  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/source/moral-algebra/s5> <https://schema.org/url> <https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/behavioural-and-cognitive-psychotherapy/article/motivational-interviewing-and-decisional-balance-contrasting-responses-to-client-ambivalence/74496E66A2D5625296F9B2EEE805B359> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/source/moral-algebra/s6> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/date> "2026-11-13"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/source/moral-algebra/s6> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/publisher> "Stanford Graduate School of Business Insights (on Snyder & Tormala, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 2017)" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/source/moral-algebra/s6> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "Want to feel more at peace with a decision? Stop dwelling on the negatives" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/source/moral-algebra/s6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#Source> .
