<https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Am I treating this thought as a fact about the world, or as something my mind is saying right now?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Is this thought helping me act on what matters here, or just making me want to escape?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Am I using a defusion trick to make the feeling go away, rather than to make room for it?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Have I checked whether the thought is pointing to a real problem that deserves action?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/check/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/example> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#Rewrite> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#after> "I'm having the thought that they'll see I'm out of my depth. That's the old not-good-enough story turning up before a big meeting, as it usually does. It can come along; I'll still make the recommendation I believe in and take the questions." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#before> "It's true, I'm out of my depth. I'll trim the proposal to the safe parts, skip the risky recommendation and read straight from the slides so nobody can ask me anything." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#situation> "Ten minutes before presenting a proposal to senior leaders, a manager keeps hearing the thought \"they're going to see I'm out of my depth\"." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#why> "The wiser response neither obeys the thought nor wastes the last ten minutes trying to argue it away; seeing it as a familiar mental event leaves the manager free to act on the judgement they actually hold." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/1> <https://schema.org/position> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/1> <https://schema.org/text> "Notice when a thought is steering you, especially a harsh verdict about yourself or a prediction of disaster that you are treating as plain fact." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/2> <https://schema.org/position> "2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/2> <https://schema.org/text> "Name it as a thought by restating it as \"I'm having the thought that…\", or label its kind, such as \"the not-good-enough story\" or \"a prediction\"." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/3> <https://schema.org/position> "3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/3> <https://schema.org/text> "Loosen its literal hold for a few seconds by saying its key word aloud quickly and repeatedly, or saying the sentence very slowly or in a silly voice, until it sounds more like sounds than a message." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/4> <https://schema.org/position> "4"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/4> <https://schema.org/text> "Let it stay without arguing with it, proving it wrong or pushing it away; acknowledge it, for example by thanking your mind for the warning." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/5> <https://schema.org/position> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/5> <https://schema.org/text> "Ask what you would do next if this thought were just background noise, and take that step while the thought comes along." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/6> <https://schema.org/position> "6"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/6> <https://schema.org/text> "Stop and seek professional support if a thought is overwhelming, persistent or about harming yourself; defusion is a self-help skill, not crisis care." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowTo> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <https://schema.org/name> "Cognitive defusion" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/1> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/2> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/3> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/4> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/5> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion/howto/6> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-16"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#Practice> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Cognitive defusion" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "defusing from thoughts" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "defusion" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "deliteralization" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "thought defusion" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/emotion-regulation> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A practice from acceptance and commitment therapy for stepping back from a painful or unhelpful thought and seeing it as a passing string of words rather than a literal truth or an order, without arguing with it or trying to make it go away, so that it has less grip on what you do next." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/scheme/self-knowledge> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/cognitive-defusion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Cognitive defusion" .
