<https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Am I doing the right thing gladly, grudgingly, or only because I would be embarrassed not to?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Is my good impulse here being guided by judgment about this situation, or just by my usual temperament?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Would I act the same way if the setting changed, if I were rushed, unobserved or in a different mood?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Which of my virtues reliably show up at work but not at home, or the other way round?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/check/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/example> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#Rewrite> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#after> "She asks what is actually blocking the work, offers a specific piece of help and a firm new date, and tells the downstream team plainly what has changed." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#before> "\"Of course, take another week.\" She absorbs the extra work herself, the downstream team slips, and the colleague never hears that the pattern is a problem." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#situation> "A team lead is generous by temperament. When a struggling colleague asks for a second extension on a deliverable that other people are waiting on, she feels the familiar pull to say yes again." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#why> "Kindness of temperament is what Aristotle would call natural virtue: a good starting point that can still misfire. It becomes a full virtue only when practical wisdom aims it at what genuinely helps everyone involved, at the right time and in the right way." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-17"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/ontology#Concept> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Moral virtues" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "character virtues" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "ethical virtues" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "ethike arete" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "virtues of character" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "ēthikē aretē" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/virtue-ethics> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Settled excellences of character, such as courage, temperance, generosity, justice and truthfulness, that dispose a person to feel and act well. Aristotle distinguished them from the intellectual virtues: they are acquired by habit rather than by teaching, and in their full form they cannot exist without practical wisdom." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/moral-virtues> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/wisdom-graph/id/scheme/virtues-and-character> .
